Novelty.evidence:
Retrieval by this refuter: 11 counted searches (5 orx discover, 3 web, 2 GitHub repo, 1 Hugging Face dataset), plus about 30 uncounted id or URL fetches (orx paper reports, GitHub API reads, 2 OpenAlex DOI resolutions). No file was edited; scratch was deleted.

Priors the proposal does not cite or credit:

1. **Colibri CNRE simulator.** https://github.com/JustVugg/colibri, docs/experiments/cnre-offline-simulator.md and c/tools/residency_sim.py; commits 2026-08-08, 08-10 and 09-20; repository has 42k stars. It does offline replay of engine routing traces through LRU, decayed LFU, SLRU, pinned+LRU and frequency-admission policies, with trace-trained and held-out category splits and per-layer miss-cost calibration from PROF logs. GLM-5.2 H200 pilot (first-party): uniform frequency admission at 120 GB gives +39.9% mean held-out felt-wait gain. Runtime: 1.66 tok/s at 57.2% hits (RAM 120 GB) against 1.31 at 73.5% (RAM 200 GB). It explicitly does not model the page cache or tok/s, and Kimi K3 traces are 'not currently safe input'. The README lists 'held-out, cross-session A/Bs across coding, chat, multilingual, and long-context' as still open.

2. **kk-r/dsv4-streaming.** https://github.com/kk-r/dsv4-streaming, created 2026-08-15, first-party, no licence. A 19-run cache sweep on an M5 Pro 64 GB (DeepSeek V4 Flash): hit rate rises 0.434 to 0.669 while tok/s falls 2.04 to 1.23. The mechanism is attributed to no F_NOCACHE, so the macOS buffer cache serves misses. Its finding that a long prefill leaves decode page-cache-cold (about 5 against 1.6 GB/s effective) maps onto the proposal's K3-type contrast. The same law replicated in ds4 at 8 GB against 32 GB.

3. **ds4 issue #810.** https://github.com/antirez/ds4/issues/810, kk-r, 2026-08-15. 'a large application cache holds copies of data that the unified buffer cache also holds'. ds4 PR #637 (https://github.com/antirez/ds4/pull/637, 2026-08-01, open) adds a Darwin measure mode with per-token page invalidation, because the page cache serves repeat expert reads.

4. **MawForge.** arXiv 2607.09686, 2026-06-17 (dossier S88). A 600-row validation matrix on an M5 Pro 24 GB, section 'Cache Hit Rate Is an Incomplete Objective'. Example: Qwen Q8 reaches 86-90% hits while decode collapses to 1.36-1.56 tok/s.

5. **danveloper/flash-moe.** https://github.com/danveloper/flash-moe, 2026-03-18 (dossier S50/S135). 'Trust the OS': the page cache gives about 71% hits, and deleting the Metal LRU gave +38%.

6. **Anemll flashmoe_cache_estimator.py.** https://github.com/Anemll/anemll-flash-llama.cpp/blob/a809594f/tools/flashmoe-sidecar/flashmoe_cache_estimator.py (commit 79b423e3, 2026-03-31; 972 lines). A published per-layer static, global static and LRU simulator over Kimi --moe-trace JSONL, with SVG output. The proposal describes the workflow but not this published tool.

Screened and not priors for Kimi K2 hit rates or a validated Mac emulator:
- MoE-CORE 2610.01950 (V4-Flash and GLM-5.2 on Ascend);
- 2610.07332 (Apple; agentic routing overlap on Qwen);
- 2609.39131, 2609.09800 HBFSim, 2610.15366, 2610.02732 ServeTwin, 2607.00466, 2609.13537, 2609.04895, 2609.38090, 2609.12978, 2608.18261;
- the Zenodo cliff Space (OLMoE);
- llama.cpp PR 29887 and its 53 comments (no Kimi);
- 0xSero REAP observations v4 (saliency only);
- sepia, expert-sniper and ssd-moe repositories (design-time bandwidth ceilings, not validated);
- Zorost (Kimi K3).

In all of this I found no K2.x multi-workload hit-rate curves and no held-out-validated Mac streaming emulator.

synthesis_result.closest_prior_mechanism_paragraph:
An open-source fork of a widely used GGUF inference engine adds SSD expert streaming for mixture-of-experts models on Apple laptops. Routed experts are extracted from the model file into a per-layer side file and served through a fixed-size per-layer slot bank with least-recently-used refill and an optional temporal prefetch. The engine can write a JSONL trace of the experts selected at each layer call. For a one-trillion-parameter model with 384 routed experts per layer and eight selected per token (a roughly 1.9-bit file, 217 GB of extracted experts), the author reports measurements on a 128 GB laptop of the current generation: one greedy prompt, 100 generated tokens, from a cold start, and mostly with routing reduced to four experts per token. At four experts, decode runs at 3.2-3.3 tokens/s for banks of 16 to 256 slots per layer, with reported per-call hit rates rising from 55.6% to 76.4% and saturating at 128 slots. At the native eight experts with 64 slots per layer, decode is 2.2 tokens/s at a 67.2% hit rate. Reads run at about 4.7 GB/s per 3.28 MB read. A companion workflow document prescribes how to estimate the benefit of a persistent expert cache for a new model:
- define the routed expert unit from exact file manifests;
- compute the resident-byte cost of per-layer banks;
- collect routing traces under realistic workloads (its worked example is one prompt generated to 12,000 tokens with end-of-sequence ignored);
- summarise unique experts per layer, expert frequencies, misses and bytes per token;
- simulate a uniform per-layer static bank, a global static set under a byte budget, and a refillable least-recently-used cache;
- convert the simulated misses into tokens per second by calibrating against runtime anchors from the same engine: the baseline streamed speed, the banked speed and an oracle all-hit replay.
The document publishes no estimator outputs, no comparison against independent measurements of other models or held-out conditions, and no sessions beyond its single prompt.
